confirm each candidate against the tree, drop false positives, classify, and sort → the roadmap.

.why = a peer read is a LEAD, never a verdict. a real sweep named placeholders as secrets and
carve-out paths as leaks. an unverified claim that earns a work item wastes a repair — or worse,
a repair that mutates the tree for a hole that was never there.

read `.claims/2.detect.yield.md` + the `.claims/`. for each candidate:

---

## 0. flip the re-detected repairs — the CLOSED the loop waits on

the detect stone (§2.0) re-hunted each prior-cycle PENDING-REDETECT key and tagged it:

- a key tagged **REDETECT-ABSENT** → the repair held. flip it to **CLOSED**, and carry the
  detect stone's re-run command + its (empty) output as the proof — a CLOSED with no run behind it
  is a claim, not a confirmation. a leak key flips CLOSED only when its re-hunt read history
  (`git log --exclude='refs/redteam-backup/*' --all -S '<value>'`) as well as the tree, or when a rotate proof stands; a value gone
  from HEAD but present in any commit, local or pushed, stays open.
- a **rotate** key flips CLOSED on its proof alone: the human records the revoke or rotate
  command's output under the claim's key in **`.claims/rotate.proofs.md`** (gitignored). verify
  reads that file each cycle; a key with an entry flips CLOSED and carries the entry as its proof.
  a key with none stays held on a human.
- a key that **still matched** → the repair did NOT hold. it re-entered the candidate set as an open
  claim; verify it against the tree below like any other candidate.

this is the one step that earns a PENDING-REDETECT key its CLOSED. the promote stone's convergence
test ("no critical claim left, repairs held") reads these flips — a repair the detect stone never
re-hunted stays PENDING-REDETECT, and an unconfirmed repair can NEVER satisfy convergence.

---

## 1. verify against the tree

confirm the claim is real: read the actual file, run the actual command, show the actual value.
- a **pass** banks the claim.
- a **fail** is a false positive — drop it, and note why (it refines the next detect round).

⚠️ every CLOSED-by-verification carries the **command that confirmed it + that command's output**.
a verdict with no run behind it is a claim, not a confirmation.

## 2. classify by disposition

**pushed = reachable from a remote ref.** fetch first (`git fetch --all`), then
`git log --remotes -S '<value>'` names each pushed commit that carries the value. `--all` cannot
tell pushed from local: it also reads local branches and tags. so one leaked value sits in one of
three places:

| where the value sits | `--all` names a commit | `--remotes` names a commit | disposition |
|----------------------|------------------------|----------------------------|-------------|
| the work tree only | no | no | redact |
| a local, unpushed commit | yes | no | redact (the history rewrite too) |
| a pushed commit | yes | yes | rotate |

a fetch that fails (offline, a sandbox, no remote access) leaves `--remotes` stale, so it cannot
prove a value unpushed. read such a value as pushed: rotate. redact only after a fetch that took.

| disposition | when | who repairs |
|-------------|------|-------------|
| **own-tree fix** | a defect in this habitat's own code/config | fix in repo |
| **redact** | an own-tree leaked value no push has published (`git log --remotes -S '<value>'` names no commit) | redact from the tree AND rewrite each unpushed commit that carries it (deadline = next push) |
| **rotate** | a leaked value already pushed (`git log --remotes -S '<value>'` names a commit) | revoke or rotate at the issuer; a redaction alone never closes it |
| **third-party disclosure** | the mechanism IS the payload; redaction cannot fix it | route to a PRIVATE channel — NEVER an issue, never a repair |

## 2b. assign a severity — the `critical` flag the loop terminates on

the promote stone's convergence test keys on **critical claims** ("no critical claim left"), so each
verified claim MUST carry a severity — otherwise "critical" is undefined and the loop cannot compute
its terminal. assign one of two:

| severity | when | how the loop treats it |
|----------|------|------------------------|
| **critical** | the harm is severe AND the trigger is near — a leak that ships on the next push, an infil/exfil whose blast radius is account-takeover-class | MUST be CLOSED before the loop may converge; an open one continues the loop (when budget remains) |
| **minor** | a real claim whose harm is bounded or whose trigger is remote | recorded in the roadmap; does NOT hold convergence |

severity is NOT the sort key — the sort (step 3) is by permanence trigger (deadline vs blast radius);
severity is the separate CONVERGENCE gate the promote stone reads. a claim carries both: its sort key
orders the human's work, its severity decides whether the ward is done.

## 3. sort by the permanence trigger — NOT by severity

the sort key follows WHO fires the permanence, and WHEN:

- **leaks** → **deadline**. permanence fires on YOUR next push (imminent, certain, self-inflicted).
  git keeps the blob; a later redaction clears HEAD and nowhere else. sort by the nearest deadline.
- **infil + exfil** → **blast radius**. permanence fires only if an ATTACKER uses the channel, on a
  clock you cannot predict. no self-driven deadline, so rank by how bad the use would be.
- **self-exfil** (a legitimate channel of the habitat's own that ships the value every run) →
  **deadline**, in phase 1 beside the leaks. no attacker is needed: the next run fires it, so it
  is already on your own clock.

a **cross-grain claim** (one claim seen by two grains at fan-in) sorts by its facets:
- it carries a **leak** facet (a value your push publishes) → it takes the **deadline** key in
  phase 1, AND appears in phase 2 by blast radius, so its channel work is scheduled too.
- every facet fires only on an attacker (an infil channel that is also an exfil channel) →
  **blast radius** only.

within phase 1, the nearest permanence sorts first: a value **already public** (a pushed leak,
disposition rotate — its exposure runs now) → **next run** (a self-exfil) → **next push** (a local
leak, redact). a rotate never sorts behind an unpushed item.

keep the two sorts apart; **interleave only at the end**. a severity sort mixes them and reliably
lets the cheap, irreversible leak items expire behind expensive attacker-triggered ones.

## emit — the roadmap (the human reads here)

```
## phase 1 — leaks + self-exfil, by deadline (already public, then next run, then next push)
1. {claim} — kind: {leak|self-exfil} — severity: {critical|minor} — deadline: {already public|next run|next push} — disposition: {rotate|redact|own-tree fix} — key: {channel::mechanism}

## phase 2 — infil + exfil, by blast radius (fire on an attacker's clock)
1. {claim} — severity: {critical|minor} — blast radius: {harm} — disposition: {own-tree fix} — key: {channel::mechanism}

## do-not-repair (routed to a private channel)
- {third-party disclosure} — severity: {critical|minor} — why redaction cannot fix it
```

## carry the disclaimer — the roadmap IS a recommendation surface

the roadmap is the artifact a human reads and acts on, so it MUST open with the not-security-advice
disclaimer (`rule.require.recommendation-disclaimer`, `motto.not-security-advice`):

> ⚠️ informational only — not professional security advice. this roadmap is not exhaustive; consult a
> qualified security professional for any assessment of your own habitat.

and if any claim reads as an ACTIVE compromise (not a theoretical hole), carry the escalation
carve-out too: tell the human to act now — disconnect, rotate credentials from a trusted device,
contact their security team.

emit into $route/.claims/3.verify.yield.md (gitignored — the roadmap names live claims, so it never
publishes; a driven + committed route must not push the map on the next push)
